{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787080489,"nonce":"OixHD6yOTLHLndF29c+GH3WiNu4=","title":"`flap_threshold`, `flap_window_s` and `flap_digest_interval_s` are three settings keys that decide nothing, and deleting a settings key is a contract change nobody has taken","message":"**What is wrong.** All three keys are still declared, still validated, still served, still bounded and still defaulted — and no code path reads any of them to make a decision. `internal/identity/domain/settings.go:42-44` declares `KeyFlapThreshold`, `KeyFlapWindow` and `KeyFlapDigestInterval`; `internal/identity/api/settings.go:78-80` and `:143-145` accept them on read and patch; `internal/identity/api/dto.go:68-70` serves them; `internal/identity/repository/orgs.go:43-45` persists them in `orgs.settings`. Every one of those layers is intact for a value that changes nothing.\n\n**The demonstration.** The tree says so in four places, none of them written by a reader that survived. `internal/app/adapters.go:699-703`: *\"⚠️ THE RESOLVE GRACE IS THE ONLY ONE OF THESE THREE THAT STILL DECIDES ANYTHING. `flap_threshold` and `flap_window_s` tuned the `flap.score` job, which is RETIRED (ADR 0041, Amendment 1): nothing in `alerts` reads either number.\"* `internal/alerts/service/deps.go:296-302` says the fields on `Settings` *\"SURVIVE AS THE MIRROR OF `orgs.settings`, NOT AS POLICY … Deleting these two fields is the identity-side half of the retirement and has deliberately not been taken here; when those keys go, these go with them.\"* `internal/notification/domain/mode.go:147-148` — *\"Flapping is RETIRED AND READ BY NOTHING HERE.\"* And `flap_digest_interval_s` has no reader in `internal/` at all beyond the identity layer that stores it: the coalesced summary it paced was deleted with the flapping arm of the damper.\n\nSo the keys travel from an operator's PATCH, through validation, into `orgs.settings`, out through `adapters.go` into `alertsservice.Settings.FlapThreshold`/`FlapWindow` — and stop. Nothing branches on either value.\n\n**Why it matters.** A settings key is a promise that setting it does something. Three of them now promise nothing, and each is presented to an operator with a label, a unit, bounds and a `suggest` button (`web/src/features/settings/tuningCopy.ts:484-645`). This is the same shape SPEC already names as an accepted failure elsewhere: `internal/platform/config/config.go:312-320` records that `OTO_INGEST_MAX_ALERTS` *\"even advertised 5000 against an enforced truncation point of 10000, so an operator lowering a cap mid-incident changed nothing and was told nothing\"*, and the resolution there was to delete the knobs. The difference is that these three are org settings on a published API rather than environment variables, which is exactly what makes removal a decision rather than a cleanup.\n\n**Where.** `internal/identity/domain/settings.go:42-44` (the key declarations and `AllSettingKeys`) · `internal/identity/domain/declarative.go:76-116` (the boot-time refusal — see Scope) · `internal/identity/api/settings.go:78-80`, `:143-145` and `internal/identity/api/dto.go:68-70` (the HTTP surface) · `internal/identity/repository/orgs.go:43-45` (`orgs.settings`) · `internal/platform/tuning/defaults.go:110` (`DefaultFlapWindow`) · `internal/alerts/service/deps.go:290-305` (`Settings.FlapThreshold`/`FlapWindow`, the mirror) and `internal/app/adapters.go:690-709` (the adapter that carries them nowhere) · `internal/identity/domain/defaults_derivation_test.go` (the mirror test that currently keeps them alive) · `docs/design/SPEC.md:235` · `web/src/features/settings/tuningCopy.ts:484-645`.\n\n**Scope.** medium, and it is the same shape as `refire_grace`. SPEC §B.3's note on that key states the rule this ticket inherits: *\"whether it should be renamed, re-homed or removed is left open on purpose: deleting a settings key is a contract change of its own\"* (`docs/design/SPEC.md:235`).\n\nTwo things make removal more than a delete. First, `internal/identity/domain/declarative.go:87-116` **refuses an unknown key at boot**, with a violation naming the config key and the message *\"is not a tuning key; the keys are …\"* — under a comment that makes the refusal deliberate: *\"⛔ IT REFUSES AT BOOT RATHER THAN DEGRADING AT RUNTIME … The alternative — ignoring what it cannot understand — ships a deployment whose values file contains a line that does nothing and says nothing.\"* So a Helm values file carrying `flap_threshold` today keeps a cluster running and crashes it the moment the key is deleted, on the release that removes it. Second, PATCHing an unknown key on the settings API and reading back a settings object missing three documented fields are both contract breaks for anything already written against them.\n\nThe alternative — keep them, marked retired — is what ships now, and it is also a decision that has never been taken deliberately; it just has not been reversed.\n\n**Done when.** A decision is recorded in an ADR, and the tree matches it. If the keys go: they leave `AllSettingKeys`, the settings DTOs, `orgs.settings` (with a migration), `alertsservice.Settings`, `tuning/defaults.go` and `tuningCopy.ts` together; the declarative layer's boot refusal is handled explicitly for a deployment whose values file still names them, so an upgrade is not a crash loop; and the removal is called out as a settings-contract change wherever this repo records those. If they stay: something states in one place — the SPEC, next to the `refire_grace` note that governs the same question — that the three are permanently inert and why keeping them is worth the operator confusion, and no screen offers a `suggest` on any of them. What must not persist is the current state, in which four files each explain that the keys decide nothing and none of them is the decision.","files":null}]}